## What I changed

The requirement: validate an E.164 telephone's national-number length against its country code — `+61` needs 9 national digits, `+1` needs 10 — and reject with 400 when wrong.

I added the check inside the shared `toE164` helper in `NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java`, right after the existing 8–15 total-digits guard:

```java
// Country-specific national-number length: '+61' requires 9 national digits,
// '+1' requires 10. Other country codes keep only the general 8-15 rule above.
if (digits.startsWith("61")) {
    if (digits.length() - 2 != 9) {
        return null;
    }
} else if (digits.startsWith("1")) {
    if (digits.length() - 1 != 10) {
        return null;
    }
}
```

Design notes:
- Putting it in `toE164` keeps normalization and the duplicate-check comparison (`RequireUniqueOwnerTelephone`) on the same canonical rules. Returning `null` makes `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` throw `OwnerTelephoneInvalidException` → 400, exactly as the malformed-number path already does.
- Only `+61` and `+1` get a specific length; other country codes (e.g. the `+64` case in `Cp08`) keep the general 8–15 rule and still pass.
- I also updated the class Javadoc to document the new rule.

Verified behavior against the tests: `+61 123` → national `123` (≠ 9) → 400 (Cp26); `0412 345 678` → `+61412345678` (9 national) still OK (Cp02/Cp08); `+64 21 123 456` unaffected (Cp08); national/international duplicates still collide (Cp03).

